questions.question_text:
Is the logarithmic curve $y=\log_{10}x$ increasing or decreasing?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}\begin{axis}[width=6.5cm,height=6.5cm,axis lines=middle,axis line style={-{Stealth}},xmin=-1,xmax=9,ymin=-4,ymax=4,xtick={2,4,6,8},ytick={-4,-2,2,4},grid=both,clip=false,scaled ticks=false,ticklabel style={font=\small,/pgf/number format/fixed},xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$}]
\addplot[thick,blue!70!black,<->,domain=0.0001:8.9,samples=100] {ln(x)/ln(10)};
\end{axis}\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

What is the equation of the vertical asymptote of $y=\log_{4}x$?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}\begin{axis}[width=6.5cm,height=6.5cm,axis lines=middle,axis line style={-{Stealth}},xmin=-1,xmax=9,ymin=-4,ymax=4,xtick={2,4,6,8},ytick={-4,-2,2,4},grid=both,clip=false,scaled ticks=false,ticklabel style={font=\small,/pgf/number format/fixed},xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$}]
\addplot[thick,blue!70!black,<->,domain=0.0039:8.9,samples=100] {ln(x)/ln(4)};
\end{axis}\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

questions.solution_text:
For $x=\tfrac{9}{25},\tfrac{3}{5},1,\tfrac{5}{3},\tfrac{25}{9}$, the values of $y$ for base $\tfrac{3}{5}$ are $2,1,0,-1,-2$, and for base $\tfrac{5}{3}$ they are $-2,-1,0,1,2$.
Plot each set of points and draw smooth curves approaching the $y$-axis without touching it.
Since $0<\tfrac{3}{5}<1$, its curve is decreasing. Since $\tfrac{5}{3}>1$, its curve is increasing.
[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}\begin{axis}[width=6.5cm,height=6.5cm,axis lines=middle,axis line style={-{Stealth}},xmin=-0.5,xmax=5,ymin=-3,ymax=3,xtick={1,2,3,4},ytick={-2,-1,1,2},grid=both,xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$}]
\addplot[thick,blue!70!black,<->,domain=0.216:4.62962963,samples=150] {ln(x)/ln(3/5)};
\addplot[thick,red!70!black,dashdotted,<->,domain=0.216:4.62962963,samples=150] {ln(x)/ln(5/3)};
\node[blue!70!black,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (axis cs:3.2,-1.5) {$y=\log_{3/5}x$};
\node[red!70!black,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (axis cs:3.2,1.5) {$y=\log_{5/3}x$};
\end{axis}\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]
